WEBVTT

1
00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.280
But I am a first year

2
00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:09.480
master's student from the University of—
National University of Singapore,

3
00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:12.520
and the paper I'm presenting today

4
00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:16.520
will be sort of part of that work,
a paper

5
00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:18.400
that I wrote for independent study.

6
00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.400
So I will

7
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.960
now share my screen.

8
00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:32.200
Okay.

9
00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:35.200
Can everyone see this?

10
00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:37.960
Okay, great.

11
00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:40.600
So I'll just start.

12
00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.080
Thank you for coming.

13
00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:47.560
Today I will present my paper titled
“Reverse Pleasure: A Defense of Difficulty

14
00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:53.240
Without Resolution.” And I'll sort of
break this down into three parts.

15
00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:58.120
The first where I will define
what I mean by difficulty, and the second

16
00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:01.880
addressing the problem of pleasure
and how it interacts with difficulty.

17
00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:06.880
And then the third, I will sort of examine
icepick largest game, Pathologic Classic

18
00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:13.480
HD, as a kind of case study to show
how pleasure might arise from what I call

19
00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:15.520
difficulty,

20
00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.480
focusing mainly on two aspects of the game

21
00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:22.200
how it deals with narrative
and how it deals with time.

22
00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:26.840
Okay,
so just to start with a broad definition

23
00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:31.120
of what I'm talking about
or with regards to difficulty,

24
00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:35.080
the OED [Oxford English Dictionary]
gives difficulty as a subject that

25
00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:39.480
needs much effort or skill to accomplish,
deal with, or understand a thing.

26
00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:43.600
And so, from just this broad definition,
we can already see how

27
00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:48.640
subjective and evaluative the term is,
where a lot of factors contribute

28
00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:54.160
to whether a player finds a game
difficult or not, and the debate between

29
00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:58.440
what should be considered too difficult
seems almost perennial.

30
00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:02.880
Like the release of song, for example,
I think is just one

31
00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:07.040
in the latest string of games
that get embroiled in quite polarized

32
00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:10.720
cultural discussions
surrounding difficulty.

33
00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:15.120
And these discussions typically center
around mechanical difficulty,

34
00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:20.080
which Patrick Jagoda defines
as the required familiarity

35
00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:23.880
with the design system
and a cultivation of skills that

36
00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:27.320
let you achieve success or mastery.

37
00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:30.720
But there are other types of difficulty
as well,

38
00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:32.880
such as interpretive and affective.

39
00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:38.640
And so my sort of project
will focus on, well,

40
00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:43.280
the intermix between the three of them,
but mainly on the second two,

41
00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:47.400
and how we justify difficulty in games

42
00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:52.120
and art in general,
because when playing a game, it's

43
00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:55.240
sort of hard
to isolate one aspect from the rest.

44
00:20:55.240 --> 00:21:00.520
And you you have to consider all of them
in holistically.

45
00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:05.960
And also when we sort of discuss
difficulty in both the public

46
00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:09.760
and academic sphere is often
not just about what

47
00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:13.840
the purpose of difficult art is,
but often touches on additional issues

48
00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:18.560
of accessibility,
aural intention, gamer culture, and so on.

49
00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:21.920
So the central question of my research

50
00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:25.520
project is basically
what is the purpose of difficulty?

51
00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:29.040
Is it a productive quality
or a barrier to entry?

52
00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:32.360
Are those
the only two ways of thinking about it?

53
00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.440
Like,
what other possibilities could difficulty

54
00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.560
open up for us
in terms of thinking about aims?

55
00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:42.720
Okay, so I'll move on to part two.

56
00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:47.360
Addressing the problem of pleasure.

57
00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:51.200
And I call it a problem
because it's kind of a hard thing to pin

58
00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:55.480
down, more so than difficulty,
I think, as most affects are.

59
00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:00.840
Leonard Diepeveen, on his work
in On Modernist Difficulty, describes it

60
00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:05.960
as this like grand but ghostly presence
that disappears under critical attention,

61
00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:09.880
and the connection
between pleasure and difficulty,

62
00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:14.280
however, has been sort of proven
by certain scholars.

63
00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:18.400
Jesper Juul, in his book
called The Art of Failure, examines

64
00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:23.440
what he calls the paradox of failure,
where players actively seek out failure

65
00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.560
even though it gives them
negative emotion,

66
00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:29.760
and he saw resolves this paradox
by showing how difficulty and failure

67
00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:34.120
make the eventual victory
more pleasurable when it is overcome.

68
00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:39.720
And when I was reading that, I sort of
had it as analogous to the perspective of

69
00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:44.880
the work of interpretation in literature,
where, like certain criticism,

70
00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:50.280
often centers around finding a like a key
to understanding a text

71
00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:55.400
or like a reading strategy or framework
that makes the text mean something.

72
00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:59.480
And so a difficult text sort of forces
the reader or critic

73
00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:03.640
to think of alternate ways
to make the text cohere, and it becomes

74
00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:07.280
like this productive force
that has to be overcome.

75
00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:10.920
And so to return
back to the context of games,

76
00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:14.200
like even in the case of, say,
like walking simulators,

77
00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:17.280
part of the pleasure,
I think, comes from unveiling

78
00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:21.520
what happens next through player action,
even if that action is

79
00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:25.720
just as simple as walking from point A
to point B to seek out the narrative.

80
00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:30.960
And so in all of these cases,
pleasure comes from making progress,

81
00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:36.000
from resolving the difficulty,
whether that be like overcoming

82
00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:40.560
like a difficult level,
or finding some sort of interpretation

83
00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:44.360
that makes a difficult
text more easy to understand.

84
00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:49.600
So, however, there are some exceptions
to this line of thinking.

85
00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:54.680
And here I'll mention Bo Ruberg,
who whose work on no-fun games

86
00:23:55.400 --> 00:24:00.280
reframes the way we think of play,
and questions like normative assumptions

87
00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.040
about what having fun even means and what

88
00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.520
the purpose of games and play really is.

89
00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:12.320
And while they are, approach
comes from the opposite end of this

90
00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:17.440
like difficulty pleasure equation,
I think we both land

91
00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:21.800
on around the same insights,
which is that pleasure can be found

92
00:24:21.800 --> 00:24:25.400
not just in progress,
but in resistance and deferred

93
00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.400
satisfaction as well.

94
00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:32.760
Okay, so in this essay,
The Pleasure of the Text,

95
00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:37.360
literary theorist Roland Barthes attempts
to locate this particular

96
00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:38.960
type of what I'm calling

97
00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:43.880
perverse pleasure by describing
what he calls the text of bliss.

98
00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:49.280
And he says it is the text
that imposes a state of loss,

99
00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:53.280
a text that discomfort unsettles
the readers historical,

100
00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:57.440
cultural, psychological assumptions,
the consistency of his tastes,

101
00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:01.640
values, memories, and brings to a crisis
his relation with language.

102
00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:06.320
In his essay, he distinguishes
between these texts and what he calls

103
00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:11.000
texts of pleasure,
which are the texts that content the grant

104
00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:15.120
euphoria, the text that comes from culture
and does not break from it.

105
00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:18.920
And so in throughout his work,
he sort of continuously circles

106
00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:22.000
around a particular poetics of pleasure.

107
00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:26.360
But I don't think he ever quite manages
the workout precisely what

108
00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:28.320
this sort of bliss entails,

109
00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:32.440
or how it emerges from the reader's
experience of discomfort.

110
00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:35.760
And in the end,
he sort of forced to conclude that

111
00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:39.360
no thesis on the pleasure of text,
the text is possible.

112
00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:43.000
And yet, in spite of everything,
the text gives me bliss.

113
00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:48.080
And so, while pleasure for him
retains that grand but ghostly presence,

114
00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:52.080
it is through this declaration of failure
that I think the pleasure of the text

115
00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:56.600
becomes itself a text of bliss,
a text that, through its omissions,

116
00:25:56.600 --> 00:26:00.440
its rhetorical complexity,
its contradictions and obsessions,

117
00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:04.200
demonstrates Barthes's argument
not so much through the content

118
00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:07.720
as the method
by which he chooses to present it,

119
00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:12.480
and so he shows that difficulty
and bliss are indeed intertwined.

120
00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.000
But the interests of bliss occurs

121
00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:18.040
in the uttering
and not in the sequence of utterances.

122
00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:22.080
So, in other words,
it's in the process of experiencing

123
00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:24.400
a difficult text
that the pleasure is found.

124
00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:27.920
And I think that's particularly the case
for video games,

125
00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:31.840
which since, like the uttering,
typically involves a higher amount

126
00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:37.000
of embodiment
than a literary like novel, for example.

127
00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:41.480
Okay,
so to that end, I'll now try to examine

128
00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:44.800
my experience
of playing Pathologic Classic HD.

129
00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.640
Henceforth,
I will refer to it as Pathologic

130
00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:53.720
and examine the ways in which 
difficulty in that game can be considered

131
00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:58.520
irresponsible in the ways
in which this bliss

132
00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:01.520
arises from that difficulty.

133
00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:05.600
So just a brief, short
background on the game.

134
00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:08.160
It was initially released in Russia

135
00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:12.120
under the title Moore, Utopia
and then was rereleased twice.

136
00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:16.040
So Classic HD,
which I played, was the second

137
00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:22.080
English translated release of the game,
and I think translation would probably be

138
00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:23.560
another layer of difficulty

139
00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:28.000
on top of everything else, but
there's probably not time to go into that,

140
00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:32.160
so I'll just focus on two 
aspects of the game.

141
00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:36.320
The first being narrative,
which is unveiled over

142
00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:40.000
the course of three
intersecting playable routes.

143
00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:43.840
Each playable character
has their own background and access

144
00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:47.880
to different types of information,
and each has 12 in-game days to attempt

145
00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:51.120
to save a fictional Russian town
for being overrun

146
00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:54.120
by this mysterious sand plague.

147
00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:57.960
So in order to save as many townspeople
as possible

148
00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:02.000
and stay alive in the process,
the player must sort of piece together

149
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:06.080
a narrative through conversations
with various NPCs, and each MPC

150
00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:10.680
has their own backstories,
motivations, biases, blind spots.

151
00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:12.160
They can lie to you.

152
00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:17.720
And so dialog is often both
quite obtuse and misleading,

153
00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:21.640
and a player can spend hours
wandering aimlessly through the town

154
00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:23.160
if they fail to follow a quest,

155
00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:27.000
or they picked the wrong dialog option
that foreclosed the quest altogether.

156
00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:30.400
And because they can lie to you,
each conversation

157
00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:33.680
becomes a struggle
in understanding, in interpretation.

158
00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:35.200
And it's this continuous

159
00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:38.760
process of interpretation
that lies at the heart of Pathologic

160
00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:42.720
gameplay, and is both what makes the game
so compelling and simultaneously,

161
00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:46.040
what makes it so difficult,
because its construction

162
00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:49.960
of narrative poses
real interpretive and affective challenges

163
00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:54.400
that then later affect how difficult
the game is mechanically as well.

164
00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:57.200
Okay.

165
00:28:57.200 --> 00:29:00.440
And then the other aspect,
the major aspect of the game

166
00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:03.040
is how it constructs time.

167
00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:08.160
So you have 12 in-game days each in game
day is about two hours real time.

168
00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:13.080
And certain quests require you
to be in certain places as specific times.

169
00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:16.920
There is no running mechanics,
so most of the game will be spent

170
00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:20.240
walking around from point
A to point B, backtracking a lot

171
00:29:20.280 --> 00:29:23.680
through locations that you've probably
already been to in the same day,

172
00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:30.760
and a large part of navigating the town,
even though you might have gone through

173
00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:35.360
the same spot multiple times, it's
still not something you can do mindlessly.

174
00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:39.400
Because as the days progress
and the plague worsens,

175
00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:43.800
you have things like marauders, soldiers,
rats, roving clouds of play

176
00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:49.040
that sort of appear and disappear
at random in infected districts.

177
00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:53.320
So you kind of got to pay attention 
and avoid those.

178
00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:56.480
Fighting in the game
is quite clunky and costly,

179
00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:59.800
and usually the safest option
is to just take a longer route

180
00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:04.040
to avoid these districts or dangers
altogether.

181
00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:08.560
And the effect of all that
is that time in the game feels often

182
00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:12.520
like it's moving both too fast
and too slow because of boredom

183
00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:17.240
of backtracking across the same streets
is cut with the anxiety of the knowledge

184
00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:21.760
that death is always around every corner
and the clock is always running down.

185
00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:26.000
Other than reading dialog
and the occasional fight,

186
00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:29.960
which you often will die
in, walking is the bulk of how you spend

187
00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:34.240
your time in the game, and in some ways
is kind of the game itself.

188
00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:38.880
Like to cut it out entirely by, say,
introducing a mod that obviously to run,

189
00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:42.600
but really to be removing to
to remove like a vital part

190
00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:44.720
of the effective experience.

191
00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:49.280
Okay, so this is just a screenshot
that I took

192
00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:53.560
while playing the game to show both show
like the visuals of the game,

193
00:30:53.560 --> 00:30:57.480
and also to sort of
make the point that the aesthetic

194
00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:01.240
of the game also contribute heavily
to how effectively difficult

195
00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:06.360
it is, because you have this consistent,
murky palette throughout that you're

196
00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:10.440
sort of seeing all the time,
and it gets exhausting after a while.

197
00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:15.960
Okay,
so I'm nearing the end of my presentation.

198
00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:19.560
There's a bit of a spoiler alert
because I will now

199
00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:24.280
talk about how I felt
after playing the end of the game.

200
00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:26.680
So at the end of my playthrough

201
00:31:26.680 --> 00:31:29.920
through “The Bachelor” route,
which is one of the characters,

202
00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:32.360
there were three endings that I could
choose from.

203
00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:36.080
I could either save the town
and destroy the Polyhedron,

204
00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:39.280
which is that little bit
coming out from the rock.

205
00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:41.520
It's like a magical building.

206
00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:42.800
Too much to explain.

207
00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:45.880
Just know that it was kind of important
throughout the story,

208
00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:51.080
so I could either save the town
and destroy the Polyhedron, or

209
00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:55.600
destroy the town and save the Polyhedron,
or let both be overrun by the plague.

210
00:31:55.600 --> 00:32:00.680
And no matter which ending I chose,
like when I went back to reload

211
00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:05.560
to see what the ending was,
it was all delivered through this sort of

212
00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:10.800
like highly theatricalized
Silent Place thing, where the town

213
00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:15.920
was extracted into a model of the town
like a rock, and the players

214
00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:21.080
and the NPCs that you had been interacting
with sort of all silently

215
00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:25.680
reacted with their really uncanny valley
looking expressions.

216
00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:31.480
And after 30 hours
and I think more close to 37 hours

217
00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:37.320
of like, struggling through this game,
these endings all felt kind of flat to me.

218
00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:40.400
There was no sense of narrative closure.

219
00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:43.720
My satisfaction was, I felt,
infinitely deferred.

220
00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:47.560
I couldn't get any sort of pleasure
from that experience

221
00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:50.240
of like having the story
finally come to a close.

222
00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:53.280
And yet, after finishing the game,

223
00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:57.800
I was still left thinking about it
long after it had the credits rolled.

224
00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:01.080
And I think part of the reason why

225
00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:04.160
it was so impactful to me

226
00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:07.440
was a combination
of experiencing the game.

227
00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:08.680
so it was

228
00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:12.560
like a combination of experiencing
the game and its unresolvable difficulty.

229
00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:17.920
I think that drove my fascination with it
and transformed my pleasure

230
00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:22.000
into something
that was not grand, but ghostly,

231
00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:26.520
but something
real with and that had teeth.

232
00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:28.360
Okay.

